    html: `
<h3>One base, one amino acid</h3>
<p>Hemoglobin, the oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells, is built from four polypeptide chains: two α chains and two β chains. The β chain has 146 amino acids. In <strong>sickle-cell anemia</strong>, the gene for the β chain has one base changed: in codon 6 of the mature protein (the 7th codon if you count the start codon, because the first methionine is removed after translation) the coding strand reads <strong>GTG instead of GAG</strong>.</p>
<figure class="diagram">
<svg viewBox="0 0 700 356" role="img" aria-labelledby="gx-sk-a-t gx-sk-a-d">
<title id="gx-sk-a-t">From one base change to a sickled cell</title>
<desc id="gx-sk-a-d">Two columns compare normal hemoglobin A with sickle hemoglobin S. DNA coding strand: GAG in HbA, GTG in HbS. mRNA: GAG versus GUG. Amino acid 6: glutamic acid, which is charged and hydrophilic, versus valine, which is nonpolar and hydrophobic. At low oxygen, HbA molecules stay separate, while HbS molecules stick together into long fibres. Red blood cells: HbA cells are flexible discs; HbS cells become stiff sickles.</desc>
<text x="300" y="30" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:14px;font-weight:700">Normal (HbA)</text>
<text x="540" y="30" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:14px;font-weight:700">Sickle cell (HbS)</text>
<text x="24" y="74" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:13px;font-weight:700">DNA (coding strand)</text>
<text x="24" y="122" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:13px;font-weight:700">mRNA</text>
<text x="24" y="172" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:13px;font-weight:700">Amino acid 6</text>
<text x="24" y="246" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:13px;font-weight:700">Hemoglobin at low O₂</text>
<text x="24" y="324" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:13px;font-weight:700">Red blood cell</text>
<text x="300" y="74" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:14px;font-weight:650">5′-…CCT GAG GAG…-3′</text>
<text x="540" y="74" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:14px;font-weight:650">5′-…CCT G<tspan style="fill:var(--bad)">T</tspan>G GAG…-3′</text>
<text x="300" y="122" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--krebs);font-size:14px;font-weight:650">5′-…CCU GAG GAG…-3′</text>
<text x="540" y="122" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--krebs);font-size:14px;font-weight:650">5′-…CCU G<tspan style="fill:var(--bad)">U</tspan>G GAG…-3′</text>
<text x="300" y="166" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:13px;font-weight:650">Glu (glutamic acid)</text>
<text x="300" y="184" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:12px">charged, hydrophilic</text>
<text x="540" y="166" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--bad);font-size:13px;font-weight:650">Val (valine)</text>
<text x="540" y="184" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:12px">nonpolar, hydrophobic</text>
<circle cx="252" cy="232" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="292" cy="252" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="334" cy="230" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="360" cy="256" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="475" cy="240" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="501" cy="240" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="527" cy="240" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="553" cy="240" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="579" cy="240" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<circle cx="605" cy="240" r="13" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<text x="300" y="282" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:12px">molecules stay separate</text>
<text x="540" y="282" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:12px">molecules stick into long fibres</text>
<ellipse cx="300" cy="320" rx="42" ry="22" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<ellipse cx="300" cy="320" rx="16" ry="8" style="fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1"/>
<path d="M496,334 Q540,282 584,334 Q540,312 496,334 Z" style="fill:var(--proton-soft);stroke:var(--proton);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<text x="350" y="346" text-anchor="start" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:12px">flexible disc</text>
<text x="592" y="346" text-anchor="start" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:12px">stiff sickle</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>The sickle-cell mutation level by level: one base substitution changes one amino acid, which changes how hemoglobin behaves at low oxygen and the shape of the cell.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>This is a textbook <strong>missense mutation</strong>: one base substituted, one amino acid changed (glutamic acid → valine, written Glu6Val). It is exactly the change shown as the "missense" example in the class mutation explorer (template CTC → CAC). Note the amino acid's name: GAG codes for <strong>glutamic acid</strong> (glutamate, Glu), not glutamine (Gln, coded by CAA or CAG).</p>
<p>The history is a landmark. In 1949 Linus Pauling showed that sickle-cell hemoglobin moves differently in an electric field, so it must carry a different charge: the first disease traced to a change in a molecule. In 1957 Vernon Ingram showed the difference was a single amino acid, the first proof that a mutation changes a protein's amino acid sequence.</p>

<h3>Why valine causes sickling</h3>
<p>Position 6 is on the outside of the hemoglobin molecule, where it touches water. Glutamic acid fits there well: its R group is <strong>negatively charged and hydrophilic</strong>. Valine's R group is <strong>nonpolar and hydrophobic</strong>. When sickle hemoglobin (HbS) gives up its oxygen in the capillaries, its shape shifts slightly, and the valine becomes a sticky patch that fits into a hydrophobic pocket on a neighbouring hemoglobin molecule. Molecules link up into long, stiff fibres that stretch the red blood cell into a sickle shape.</p>
<p>The consequences follow from the shape:</p>
<ul>
<li>Sickled cells are <strong>fragile</strong> and are destroyed after 10–20 days instead of the normal 120, causing <strong>anemia</strong> (too few red blood cells), tiredness and jaundice.</li>
<li>Stiff cells <strong>block small blood vessels</strong>, causing sudden attacks of severe pain ("crises"), strokes, and damage to the spleen, lungs, kidneys and bones.</li>
<li>Without treatment, many children with sickle-cell anemia in low-income countries die before age 5.</li>
</ul>
<p>A different mutation at the same codon, GAG → <strong>AAG</strong> (Glu → Lys, hemoglobin C), causes only mild disease. Lysine is still charged and hydrophilic, just positive instead of negative, so it does not create a sticky patch. Same position, same type of mutation (missense), very different outcome: the effect depends on the chemistry of the new amino acid.</p>

<h3>Inheritance: disease and trait</h3>
<p>Each person has two copies of the β-globin gene, one from each parent. Write the normal allele as A (for HbA) and the sickle allele as S.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>AA</strong>: normal hemoglobin.</li>
<li><strong>AS</strong>: <strong>sickle-cell trait</strong>. Half their hemoglobin is normal, which is enough to keep cells from sickling under normal conditions. Carriers are usually healthy.</li>
<li><strong>SS</strong>: <strong>sickle-cell disease</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<figure class="diagram narrow">
<svg viewBox="0 0 440 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="gx-sk-b-t gx-sk-b-d">
<title id="gx-sk-b-t">Punnett square for two sickle-cell carriers</title>
<desc id="gx-sk-b-d">Both parents are AS carriers. Their children have a 1 in 4 chance of AA (normal hemoglobin but susceptible to malaria), a 2 in 4 chance of AS (sickle-cell trait, protected from severe malaria) and a 1 in 4 chance of SS (sickle-cell disease).</desc>
<text x="270" y="22" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:14px;font-weight:700">Two carriers: AS × AS</text>
<text x="200" y="50" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:700">A</text>
<text x="340" y="50" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:700">S</text>
<text x="114" y="100" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:700">A</text>
<text x="114" y="170" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:700">S</text>
<text x="12" y="140" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:12px">parent 2</text>
<text x="424" y="50" text-anchor="end" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:12px">parent 1</text>
<rect x="130" y="60" width="140" height="70" style="fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<text x="200" y="84" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:700">AA</text>
<text x="200" y="104" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:11px">normal, but</text>
<text x="200" y="119" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:11px">malaria-susceptible</text>
<rect x="270" y="60" width="140" height="70" style="fill:var(--good-soft);stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<text x="340" y="84" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:700">AS</text>
<text x="340" y="104" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:11px">sickle-cell trait:</text>
<text x="340" y="119" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:11px">malaria-protected</text>
<rect x="130" y="130" width="140" height="70" style="fill:var(--good-soft);stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<text x="200" y="154" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:700">AS</text>
<text x="200" y="174" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:11px">sickle-cell trait:</text>
<text x="200" y="189" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:11px">malaria-protected</text>
<rect x="270" y="130" width="140" height="70" style="fill:var(--bad-soft);stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5"/>
<text x="340" y="154" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:15px;font-weight:700">SS</text>
<text x="340" y="174" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:11px">sickle-cell</text>
<text x="340" y="189" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink-2);font-size:11px">disease</text>
<text x="270" y="228" text-anchor="middle" style="fill:var(--ink);font-size:12px">1 AA : 2 AS : 1 SS (each child: 25% chance of SS)</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>Two carriers: each child has a 25% chance of sickle-cell disease and a 50% chance of being a carrier.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The mutation is in the germ line, so it is <strong>heritable</strong>: it is in every cell of the body, including the stem cells in bone marrow that make red blood cells.</p>

<h3>The malaria connection</h3>
<p>If SS is so harmful, why is the S allele common? In parts of sub-Saharan Africa, 10–40% of people carry it, and it is also found in the Middle East, India and the Mediterranean. Those are the regions where <strong>malaria</strong>, caused by the parasite <em>Plasmodium falciparum</em> and spread by mosquitoes, has been common for thousands of years.</p>
<p>Malaria parasites spend part of their life inside red blood cells. In a carrier (AS), an infected red blood cell is more likely to sickle, because the parasite uses up oxygen inside it. Sickled, infected cells are removed by the spleen, taking the parasites with them, and the parasites also grow poorly in cells with HbS. Carriers are about 90% protected against severe, life-threatening malaria.</p>
<p>So in a malaria region:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>AA</strong> children are at high risk of dying of malaria;</li>
<li><strong>SS</strong> children are at high risk of dying of sickle-cell disease;</li>
<li><strong>AS</strong> children have the best chance of surviving to have children of their own.</li>
</ul>
<p>Natural selection therefore keeps <em>both</em> alleles in the population: this is called <strong>heterozygote advantage</strong>, and the stable mix of alleles is a <strong>balanced polymorphism</strong>. It is the clearest example of the slide's point that a mutation can have a negative effect, a positive effect, or both, depending on the environment. Among people of African descent in North America, where malaria is absent, the S allele brings only the cost, and its frequency is slowly falling.</p>

<h3>Treatments, from hydroxyurea to CRISPR</h3>
<p>Before birth, humans make a different hemoglobin, <strong>fetal hemoglobin</strong> (HbF), which uses γ chains instead of β chains and so contains no HbS. After birth the γ gene is switched off and the β gene on. Babies with SS are healthy until about six months, when HbF falls. Treatments use this:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hydroxyurea</strong>, an inexpensive pill, switches some HbF production back on and reduces crises.</li>
<li><strong>Bone marrow transplant</strong> from a matched donor can cure the disease, but matched donors are hard to find.</li>
<li><strong>Gene therapy</strong>: in 2023–2024, the first CRISPR-based treatment (Casgevy) was approved in the UK, the US and Canada. Doctors remove the patient's own blood stem cells, use CRISPR to disrupt a DNA switch that normally keeps the fetal γ gene off, and return the cells. The edited cells make enough HbF to prevent sickling. See the CRISPR topic for how the cut is made.</li>
</ul>

<details class="check"><summary>Why is sickle-cell anemia described as a missense mutation and not a frameshift, even though the protein's behaviour changes so much?</summary><div class="ans"><p>The mutation is a single base substitution (A → T in the coding strand) that changes one codon (GAG → GUG) and therefore one amino acid (Glu → Val). The reading frame is unchanged, and every other amino acid in the 146-amino-acid chain is normal. The large effect comes from where the new amino acid is and how different its chemistry is, not from the size of the DNA change.</p></div></details>
<details class="check"><summary>Two carriers (AS) have a child. What is the chance the child has sickle-cell disease, and what is the chance the child is a carrier?</summary><div class="ans"><p>From the Punnett square: 1/4 (25%) SS, sickle-cell disease; 2/4 (50%) AS, carrier; 1/4 (25%) AA. These chances apply to each child independently.</p></div></details>
`
